 "survey": {
  "notes": "Sheet shows bilge system wiring: relays Re1-Re6 driving bilge valves and status/switch inputs across a terminal strip (T/S C, T/S B, T/S S), feeding into a Main Panel (Plug C, Plug D) and a Main Panel PC Card with open/closed indicator lamps for each bilge compartment. Left side shows +24V service battery supply through F2 200A and F3 60A to SW8/SO6 and Q13 10A breaker feeding relay coil bank. Right side top shows Main Bilge Pump motor fed from F3/SW8 line. Numerous wire-gauge diamond callouts ([2],[3] etc) on valve/motor conductors are wire-size annotations, not devices. XA40, XA10, XA11 tags are cross-reference/status tap points routing to remote panel (to ONYX noted near bottom, and cross ref to Main Panel). Terminal strip T/S C carries open/close contact pairs (OPEN/CLOSE toggle symbols) for 5 valve/motor groups plus bilge switch (BILGE SW) taps for Port/Stbd Lazarette, Aft, Eng Room, Fwd, Sail Locker compartments. Bottom shows title block with drawing no, title, date, drawn, project, customer fields; some text at small size illegible (e.g. \"SO\" prefix legend, some plug pin labels).",
  "sub_type": "relay_terminal_wiring",
  "sub_type_reason": "Dominant structure is terminal strips (T/S S, T/S B, T/S C) interconnected with relay bank (Re1-Re6) driving bilge valve motors and switches, with signal taps (XA40/XA10/XA11) routed to a monitoring/alarm system (Main Panel PC Card with open/closed indicator lamps) - classic relay/terminal wiring layout rather than a one-line power topology or a simple distribution schedule.",
